#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504780 :question/problem "Given that $-16a - 12b$ and $λ(20a + 15b)$ are equal (non-zero) vectors, find the scale factor $λ$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n$$\nλ(20a + 15b)=λ(20)a+λ(15)b.\n$$\n\nThe same scale factor must multiply the whole non-zero vector. Comparing the two coefficients gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ &= \\frac{-16}{20} = -\\frac{4}{5} \\\\\nλ &= \\frac{-12}{15} = -\\frac{4}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n-16a - 12b=-\\frac{4}{5}(20a + 15b).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nλ=-\\frac{4}{5}\n$$\n\nThe two non-zero vectors are parallel. Their directions are opposite because the factor is negative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504802 :question/problem "Given that $λ(3a + 4b)$ and $12a + 16b$ are equal (non-zero) vectors, find the scale factor $λ$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n$$\nλ(3a + 4b)=λ(3)a+λ(4)b.\n$$\n\nThe same scale factor must multiply the whole non-zero vector. Comparing the two coefficients gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ &= \\frac{12}{3} = 4 \\\\\nλ &= \\frac{16}{4} = 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n12a + 16b=4(3a + 4b).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nλ=4\n$$\n\nThe two non-zero vectors are parallel. Their directions are the same because the factor is positive."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504803 :question/problem "Given that $λ(3a - 4b)$ and $18a - 24b$ are equal (non-zero) vectors, find the scale factor $λ$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n$$\nλ(3a - 4b)=λ(3)a+λ(-4)b.\n$$\n\nThe same scale factor must multiply the whole non-zero vector. Comparing the two coefficients gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ &= \\frac{18}{3} = 6 \\\\\nλ &= \\frac{-24}{-4} = 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n18a - 24b=6(3a - 4b).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nλ=6\n$$\n\nThe two non-zero vectors are parallel. Their directions are the same because the factor is positive."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504821 :question/problem "Given that $24a - 8b$ and $λ(3a - b)$ are equal (non-zero) vectors, find the scale factor $λ$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n$$\nλ(3a - b)=λ(3)a+λ(-1)b.\n$$\n\nThe same scale factor must multiply the whole non-zero vector. Comparing the two coefficients gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ &= \\frac{24}{3} = 8 \\\\\nλ &= \\frac{-8}{-1} = 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n24a - 8b=8(3a - b).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nλ=8\n$$\n\nThe two non-zero vectors are parallel. Their directions are the same because the factor is positive."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504832 :question/problem "Given that $λ(0.5a + 3b)$ and $-a - 6b$ are equal (non-zero) vectors, find the scale factor $λ$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n$$\nλ(\\frac{1}{2}a + 3b)=λ\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)a+λ(3)b.\n$$\n\nThe same scale factor must multiply the whole non-zero vector. Comparing the two coefficients gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ &= \\frac{-1}{\\frac{1}{2}} = -2 \\\\\nλ &= \\frac{-6}{3} = -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n-a - 6b=-2(\\frac{1}{2}a + 3b).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nλ=-2\n$$\n\nThe two non-zero vectors are parallel. Their directions are opposite because the factor is negative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504834 :question/problem "Given that $4a - 8b$ and $λ(a - 2b)$ are equal (non-zero) vectors, find the scale factor $λ$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n$$\nλ(a - 2b)=λ(1)a+λ(-2)b.\n$$\n\nThe same scale factor must multiply the whole non-zero vector. Comparing the two coefficients gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ &= \\frac{4}{1} = 4 \\\\\nλ &= \\frac{-8}{-2} = 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n4a - 8b=4(a - 2b).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nλ=4\n$$\n\nThe two non-zero vectors are parallel. Their directions are the same because the factor is positive."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504837 :question/problem "Given that $λ(3a + 12b)$ and $-5a - 20b$ are equal (non-zero) vectors, find the scale factor $λ$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n$$\nλ(3a + 12b)=λ(3)a+λ(12)b.\n$$\n\nThe same scale factor must multiply the whole non-zero vector. Comparing the two coefficients gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ &= \\frac{-5}{3} = -\\frac{5}{3} \\\\\nλ &= \\frac{-20}{12} = -\\frac{5}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n-5a - 20b=-\\frac{5}{3}(3a + 12b).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nλ=-\\frac{5}{3}\n$$\n\nThe two non-zero vectors are parallel. Their directions are opposite because the factor is negative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504838 :question/problem "Given that $3a + 24b$ and $λ(0.5a + 4b)$ are equal (non-zero) vectors, find the scale factor $λ$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n$$\nλ(\\frac{1}{2}a + 4b)=λ\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)a+λ(4)b.\n$$\n\nThe same scale factor must multiply the whole non-zero vector. Comparing the two coefficients gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ &= \\frac{3}{\\frac{1}{2}} = 6 \\\\\nλ &= \\frac{24}{4} = 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n3a + 24b=6(\\frac{1}{2}a + 4b).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nλ=6\n$$\n\nThe two non-zero vectors are parallel. Their directions are the same because the factor is positive."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504839 :question/problem "Given that $-a - 2b$ and $λ(0.5a + b)$ are equal (non-zero) vectors, find the scale factor $λ$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n$$\nλ(\\frac{1}{2}a + b)=λ\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)a+λ(1)b.\n$$\n\nThe same scale factor must multiply the whole non-zero vector. Comparing the two coefficients gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ &= \\frac{-1}{\\frac{1}{2}} = -2 \\\\\nλ &= \\frac{-2}{1} = -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n-a - 2b=-2(\\frac{1}{2}a + b).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nλ=-2\n$$\n\nThe two non-zero vectors are parallel. Their directions are opposite because the factor is negative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504841 :question/problem "Given that $-24a + 6b$ and $λ(4a - b)$ are equal (non-zero) vectors, find the scale factor $λ$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n$$\nλ(4a - b)=λ(4)a+λ(-1)b.\n$$\n\nThe same scale factor must multiply the whole non-zero vector. Comparing the two coefficients gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ &= \\frac{-24}{4} = -6 \\\\\nλ &= \\frac{6}{-1} = -6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n-24a + 6b=-6(4a - b).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nλ=-6\n$$\n\nThe two non-zero vectors are parallel. Their directions are opposite because the factor is negative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504863 :question/problem "![](images/ae/ae3cb3c0a30251ff66193040df7d2f564e771ba60ec711c915099ee2056bfcb5.png)\n\nConsider the diagram that is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "- Statement $b =-2e$ is false. The vectors $b$ and $e$ have the same direction. So, $b\\neq-2e$.\n- Statement $c =-d$ is false. The vectors $c$ and $d$ have the same direction. So, $c\\neq-d$.\n- Statement $b = 2e$ is true. Indeed, vectors $e$ and $b$ are parallel and have the same direction. Also, the magnitude of $b$ is $2$ times the magnitude of $e$. So, $b=2e$.\n\nTherefore, only statement $b = 2e$ is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504870 :question/problem "![](images/cf/cf44c063e6d2f5dc58e33d0007277696e99cac5efecffa0fc022399e77ed6a79.png)\n\nConsider the diagram that is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "- Statement $b =-\\frac{2}{3}e$ is false. The vectors $b$ and $e$ have the same direction. So, $b\\neq-\\frac{2}{3}e$.\n- Statement $c =-d$ is false. The vectors $c$ and $d$ have the same direction. So, $c\\neq-d$.\n- Statement $b = \\frac{2}{3}e$ is true. Indeed, vectors $e$ and $b$ are parallel and have the same direction. Also, the magnitude of $b$ is $\\frac{2}{3}$ times the magnitude of $e$. So, $b=\\frac{2}{3}e$.\n\nTherefore, only statement $b = \\frac{2}{3}e$ is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504884 :question/problem "![](images/5f/5fb207920b98f144e2cfea37c65fc82ec863fcb536511611591a2bef61c25b0f.png)\n\nConsider the diagram that is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "- Statement $b =-2e$ is false. The vectors $b$ and $e$ have the same direction. So, $b\\neq-2e$.\n- Statement $c =-d$ is false. The vectors $c$ and $d$ have the same direction. So, $c\\neq-d$.\n- Statement $b = 2e$ is true. Indeed, vectors $e$ and $b$ are parallel and have the same direction. Also, the magnitude of $b$ is $2$ times the magnitude of $e$. So, $b=2e$.\n\nTherefore, only statement $b = 2e$ is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504903 :question/problem "![](images/bf/bf995386f81f8768208476add2ab8e29105583a2d337c46e9279b98259669d8f.png)\n\nConsider the diagram that is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "- Statement $b =-e$ is false. The vectors $b$ and $e$ have the same direction. So, $b\\neq-e$.\n- Statement $c =-d$ is false. The vectors $c$ and $d$ have the same direction. So, $c\\neq-d$.\n- Statement $b = e$ is true. Indeed, vectors $e$ and $b$ are parallel and have the same direction. Also, the magnitude of $b$ is $1$ times the magnitude of $e$. So, $b=e$.\n\nTherefore, only statement $b = e$ is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504905 :question/problem "![](images/cc/cc3b45df80219b1d5b710a7372efc8929e4b91ad596da62c2f4592c62eebf656.png)\n\nConsider the diagram that is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "- Statement $b =-2e$ is false. The vectors $b$ and $e$ have the same direction. So, $b\\neq-2e$.\n- Statement $c =-d$ is true. Indeed, vectors $d$ and $c$ are parallel and have opposite directions. Also, the magnitude of $c$ is $1$ times the magnitude of $d$. So, $c=-d$.\n- Statement $b = 2e$ is true. Indeed, vectors $e$ and $b$ are parallel and have the same direction. Also, the magnitude of $b$ is $2$ times the magnitude of $e$. So, $b=2e$.\n\nTherefore, only statements $c =-d$ and $b = 2e$ are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504923 :question/problem "![](images/06/06c81360d1abb2e99648b34cf19877be94719a48abe377d2143cf9ac06886814.png)\n\nConsider the diagram that is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "- Statement $b =-e$ is false. The vectors $b$ and $e$ have the same direction. So, $b\\neq-e$.\n- Statement $c =-d$ is true. Indeed, vectors $d$ and $c$ are parallel and have opposite directions. Also, the magnitude of $c$ is $1$ times the magnitude of $d$. So, $c=-d$.\n- Statement $b = e$ is true. Indeed, vectors $e$ and $b$ are parallel and have the same direction. Also, the magnitude of $b$ is $1$ times the magnitude of $e$. So, $b=e$.\n\nTherefore, only statements $c =-d$ and $b = e$ are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504924 :question/problem "![](images/26/26b8e79180d5c7fa12105953fb43df605a6699a16d6708f537a1bb154999eace.png)\n\nConsider the diagram that is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "- Statement $a =-c$ is false. The magnitude of $a$ is $2$ times the magnitude of $c$. So, $a\\neq-c$.\n- Statement $c =-\\frac{1}{2}a$ is true. Indeed, vectors $a$ and $c$ are parallel and have opposite directions. Also, the magnitude of $c$ is $\\frac{1}{2}$ times the magnitude of $a$. So, $c=-\\frac{1}{2}a$.\n- Statement $b = d$ is true. Indeed, vectors $d$ and $b$ are parallel and have the same direction. Also, the magnitude of $b$ is $1$ times the magnitude of $d$. So, $b=d$.\n\nTherefore, only statements $c =-\\frac{1}{2}a$ and $b = d$ are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504931 :question/problem "![](images/10/10abc4680454b89e41467e52560a6ad4f36f2568e141bc6814da47fc86f8c98a.png)\n\nConsider the diagram that is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "- Statement $b =-e$ is true. Indeed, vectors $e$ and $b$ are parallel and have opposite directions. Also, the magnitude of $b$ is $1$ times the magnitude of $e$. So, $b=-e$.\n- Statement $c =-d$ is false. The vectors $c$ and $d$ have the same direction. So, $c\\neq-d$.\n- Statement $b = e$ is false. The vectors $b$ and $e$ have opposite directions. So, $b\\neq e$.\n\nTherefore, only statement $b =-e$ is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504932 :question/problem "![](images/d3/d36fa8ce7f7f40ce3380fb5d8d0477a6ab9dfd10fa110da7cb71abb155fdab55.png)\n\nConsider the diagram that is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "- Statement $a =-c$ is false. The magnitude of $a$ is $\\frac{2}{3}$ times the magnitude of $c$. So, $a\\neq-c$.\n- Statement $c =-\\frac{3}{2}a$ is true. Indeed, vectors $a$ and $c$ are parallel and have opposite directions. Also, the magnitude of $c$ is $\\frac{3}{2}$ times the magnitude of $a$. So, $c=-\\frac{3}{2}a$.\n- Statement $b = d$ is true. Indeed, vectors $d$ and $b$ are parallel and have the same direction. Also, the magnitude of $b$ is $1$ times the magnitude of $d$. So, $b=d$.\n\nTherefore, only statements $c =-\\frac{3}{2}a$ and $b = d$ are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504958 :question/problem "![](images/b4/b49501c42dddb61bc8cbc33ded323defc501978ddb6d880b7fcfe0d48a809ba3.png)\n\nConsider the diagram that is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "- Statement $b =-e$ is false. The vectors $b$ and $e$ have the same direction. So, $b\\neq-e$.\n- Statement $c =-d$ is false. The vectors $c$ and $d$ have the same direction. So, $c\\neq-d$.\n- Statement $b = e$ is true. Indeed, vectors $e$ and $b$ are parallel and have the same direction. Also, the magnitude of $b$ is $1$ times the magnitude of $e$. So, $b=e$.\n\nTherefore, only statement $b = e$ is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504973 :question/problem "![](images/4a/4ae57d39834857c91768164e64da6ad111abc8a02c2815e4792dff4a9572a175.png)\n\nConsider the diagram that is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "- Statement $a =-c$ is false. The magnitude of $a$ is $2$ times the magnitude of $c$. So, $a\\neq-c$.\n- Statement $c =-\\frac{1}{2}a$ is false. The vectors $c$ and $a$ have the same direction. So, $c\\neq-\\frac{1}{2}a$.\n- Statement $b = d$ is true. Indeed, vectors $d$ and $b$ are parallel and have the same direction. Also, the magnitude of $b$ is $1$ times the magnitude of $d$. So, $b=d$.\n\nTherefore, only statement $b = d$ is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504978 :question/problem "![](images/74/744ee1d9ed73bced615707dd3098247fe157377545d10d64df6baa68c491bea6.png)\n\nConsider the diagram that is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "- Statement $b =-\\frac{2}{3}e$ is true. Indeed, vectors $e$ and $b$ are parallel and have opposite directions. Also, the magnitude of $b$ is $\\frac{2}{3}$ times the magnitude of $e$. So, $b=-\\frac{2}{3}e$.\n- Statement $c =-d$ is false. The vectors $c$ and $d$ have the same direction. So, $c\\neq-d$.\n- Statement $b = \\frac{2}{3}e$ is false. The vectors $b$ and $e$ have opposite directions. So, $b\\neq\\frac{2}{3}e$.\n\nTherefore, only statement $b =-\\frac{2}{3}e$ is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504979 :question/problem "![](images/9e/9e34143ff6568821f3ea8ce4dd3f768b33798cdff6403b8b42d2a56e0c764a9e.png)\n\nConsider the diagram that is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "- Statement $a =-c$ is false. The magnitude of $a$ is $\\frac{2}{3}$ times the magnitude of $c$. So, $a\\neq-c$.\n- Statement $c =-\\frac{3}{2}a$ is false. The vectors $c$ and $a$ have the same direction. So, $c\\neq-\\frac{3}{2}a$.\n- Statement $b = d$ is true. Indeed, vectors $d$ and $b$ are parallel and have the same direction. Also, the magnitude of $b$ is $1$ times the magnitude of $d$. So, $b=d$.\n\nTherefore, only statement $b = d$ is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504984 :question/problem "![](images/a7/a79b77752dd8697fe6b08f7546c3bfb09618e155e9560b82d9b5475dcd54b34c.png)\n\nConsider the diagram that is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "- Statement $b =-e$ is false. The vectors $b$ and $e$ have the same direction. So, $b\\neq-e$.\n- Statement $c =-d$ is false. The vectors $c$ and $d$ have the same direction. So, $c\\neq-d$.\n- Statement $b = e$ is true. Indeed, vectors $e$ and $b$ are parallel and have the same direction. Also, the magnitude of $b$ is $1$ times the magnitude of $e$. So, $b=e$.\n\nTherefore, only statement $b = e$ is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504994 :question/problem "![](images/a0/a08d3177f41181b55a923a7e13041118f15e807e01667ad6d5e0cd95d27a6b83.png)\n\nConsider the diagram that is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "- Statement $b =-\\frac{2}{3}e$ is false. The vectors $b$ and $e$ have the same direction. So, $b\\neq-\\frac{2}{3}e$.\n- Statement $c =-d$ is false. The vectors $c$ and $d$ have the same direction. So, $c\\neq-d$.\n- Statement $b = \\frac{2}{3}e$ is true. Indeed, vectors $e$ and $b$ are parallel and have the same direction. Also, the magnitude of $b$ is $\\frac{2}{3}$ times the magnitude of $e$. So, $b=\\frac{2}{3}e$.\n\nTherefore, only statement $b = \\frac{2}{3}e$ is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505001 :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6x - 8y - 31 &=0 \\\\ 2x - 4y - 6 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Sometimes, it's handy to assign a number to each equation in a system, so that we can refer to them more easily:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6x - 8y - 31 &=0 && (1) \\\\ 2x - 4y - 6 &=0 && (2)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. Using equation $\\color{blue}(2)$, we can solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 2x - 4y - 6 &= 0 \\\\ 2x - 4y &= 6 \\\\ 2x &= 6 + 4y \\\\ x &= \\dfrac{6 + 4y}{2} \\\\ x &= 3 + 2y \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=3+2y$ into equation $\\color{red}(1)$ and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 6x - 8y - 31 &= 0 \\\\ 6(3 + 2y) - 8y - 31 &= 0 \\\\ 18 + 12y - 8y - 31 &= 0 \\\\ -13 + 4y &= 0 \\\\ 4y &= 13 \\\\ y &= \\dfrac{13}{4} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{13}{4}\n$$\n\ninto equation $\\color{red}(1)$, then we can find the value of $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 6x - 8y - 31 &= 0 \\\\ 6x - 8\\left(\\dfrac{13}{4}\\right) - 31 &= 0 \\\\ 6x - 26 - 31 &= 0 \\\\ 6x - 57 &= 0 \\\\ 6x &= 57 \\\\ x &= \\dfrac{19}{2} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=\\dfrac{19}{2}$ and\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{13}{4}\n$$\n\n, which can be written as\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{19}{2},\\dfrac{13}{4}\\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505002 :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}7a - 8b + 41 &=0 \\\\ 4a - 4b + 20 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Sometimes, it's handy to assign a number to each equation in a system, so that we can refer to them more easily:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}7a - 8b + 41 &=0 && (1) \\\\ 4a - 4b + 20 &=0 && (2)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. Using equation $\\color{blue}(2)$, we can solve for $a$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 4a - 4b + 20 &= 0 \\\\ 4a - 4b &= -20 \\\\ 4a &= -20 + 4b \\\\ a &= \\dfrac{-20 + 4b}{4} \\\\ a &= -5 + b \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $a=-5+b$ into equation $\\color{red}(1)$ and solve for $b$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 7a - 8b + 41 &= 0 \\\\ 7(-5 + b) - 8b + 41 &= 0 \\\\ -35 + 7b - 8b + 41 &= 0 \\\\ 6 - b &= 0 \\\\ -b &= -6 \\\\ b &= 6 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute\n\n$$\nb=6\n$$\n\ninto equation $\\color{red}(1)$, then we can find the value of $a$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 7a - 8b + 41 &= 0 \\\\ 7a - 8\\left(6\\right) + 41 &= 0 \\\\ 7a - 48 + 41 &= 0 \\\\ 7a - 7 &= 0 \\\\ 7a &= 7 \\\\ a &= 1 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=1$ and\n\n$$\nb=6\n$$\n\n, which can be written as\n\n$$\n\\left(1,6\\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505004 :question/problem "Using the method of substitution, find the solution of\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x + y &=-7 \\\\ 2x - 3y &=13\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$." :question/worked-solution "Sometimes, it's handy to assign a number to each equation in a system, so that we can refer to them more easily:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x + y + 7 &=0 && (1) \\\\ 2x - 3y - 13 &=0 && (2)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. Using equation $\\color{blue}(1)$, we can solve for $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} y + 2x + 7 &= 0 \\\\ y + 2x &= -7 \\\\ y &= -7 - 2x \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y=-7-2x$ into equation $\\color{red}(2)$ and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} -3y + 2x - 13 &= 0 \\\\ -3(-7 - 2x) + 2x - 13 &= 0 \\\\ 21 + 6x + 2x - 13 &= 0 \\\\ 8 + 8x &= 0 \\\\ 8x &= -8 \\\\ x &= -1 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute\n\n$$\nx=-1\n$$\n\ninto equation $\\color{red}(2)$, then we can find the value of $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} -3y + 2x - 13 &= 0 \\\\ -3y + 2\\left(-1\\right) - 13 &= 0 \\\\ -3y - 2 - 13 &= 0 \\\\ -3y - 15 &= 0 \\\\ -3y &= 15 \\\\ y &= -5 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $y=-5$ and\n\n$$\nx=-1\n$$\n\n, which can be written as\n\n$$\n\\left(-1,-5\\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505005 :question/problem "Using the method of substitution, find the solution of\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-6p - 9q &=-6 \\\\ 3p + 3q &=6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Sometimes, it's handy to assign a number to each equation in a system, so that we can refer to them more easily:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-6p - 9q + 6 &=0 && (1) \\\\ 3p + 3q - 6 &=0 && (2)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. Using equation $\\color{blue}(2)$, we can solve for $q$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 3q + 3p - 6 &= 0 \\\\ 3q + 3p &= 6 \\\\ 3q &= 6 - 3p \\\\ q &= \\dfrac{6 - 3p}{3} \\\\ q &= 2 - p \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $q=2-p$ into equation $\\color{red}(1)$ and solve for $p$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} -9q - 6p + 6 &= 0 \\\\ -9(2 - p) - 6p + 6 &= 0 \\\\ -18 + 9p - 6p + 6 &= 0 \\\\ -12 + 3p &= 0 \\\\ 3p &= 12 \\\\ p &= 4 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute\n\n$$\np=4\n$$\n\ninto equation $\\color{red}(1)$, then we can find the value of $q$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} -9q - 6p + 6 &= 0 \\\\ -9q - 6\\left(4\\right) + 6 &= 0 \\\\ -9q - 24 + 6 &= 0 \\\\ -9q - 18 &= 0 \\\\ -9q &= 18 \\\\ q &= -2 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $q=-2$ and\n\n$$\np=4\n$$\n\n, which can be written as\n\n$$\n\\left(4,-2\\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505044 :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6p - 4q + 68 &=0 \\\\ 4p + 4q + 12 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Sometimes, it's handy to assign a number to each equation in a system, so that we can refer to them more easily:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6p - 4q + 68 &=0 && (1) \\\\ 4p + 4q + 12 &=0 && (2)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. Using equation $\\color{blue}(2)$, we can solve for $p$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 4p + 4q + 12 &= 0 \\\\ 4p + 4q &= -12 \\\\ 4p &= -12 - 4q \\\\ p &= \\dfrac{-12 - 4q}{4} \\\\ p &= -3 - q \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $p=-3-q$ into equation $\\color{red}(1)$ and solve for $q$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 6p - 4q + 68 &= 0 \\\\ 6(-3 - q) - 4q + 68 &= 0 \\\\ -18 - 6q - 4q + 68 &= 0 \\\\ 50 - 10q &= 0 \\\\ -10q &= -50 \\\\ q &= 5 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute\n\n$$\nq=5\n$$\n\ninto equation $\\color{red}(1)$, then we can find the value of $p$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 6p - 4q + 68 &= 0 \\\\ 6p - 4\\left(5\\right) + 68 &= 0 \\\\ 6p - 20 + 68 &= 0 \\\\ 6p + 48 &= 0 \\\\ 6p &= -48 \\\\ p &= -8 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $p=-8$ and\n\n$$\nq=5\n$$\n\n, which can be written as\n\n$$\n\\left(-8,5\\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505050 :question/problem "Using the method of substitution, find the solution of\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5x + 2y &=17 \\\\ 3x + 3y &=6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Sometimes, it's handy to assign a number to each equation in a system, so that we can refer to them more easily:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5x + 2y - 17 &=0 && (1) \\\\ 3x + 3y - 6 &=0 && (2)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. Using equation $\\color{blue}(2)$, we can solve for $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 3y + 3x - 6 &= 0 \\\\ 3y + 3x &= 6 \\\\ 3y &= 6 - 3x \\\\ y &= \\dfrac{6 - 3x}{3} \\\\ y &= 2 - x \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y=2-x$ into equation $\\color{red}(1)$ and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 2y + 5x - 17 &= 0 \\\\ 2(2 - x) + 5x - 17 &= 0 \\\\ 4 - 2x + 5x - 17 &= 0 \\\\ -13 + 3x &= 0 \\\\ 3x &= 13 \\\\ x &= \\dfrac{13}{3} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{13}{3}\n$$\n\ninto equation $\\color{red}(1)$, then we can find the value of $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 2y + 5x - 17 &= 0 \\\\ 2y + 5\\left(\\dfrac{13}{3}\\right) - 17 &= 0 \\\\ 2y + \\dfrac{65}{3} - 17 &= 0 \\\\ 2y + \\dfrac{14}{3} &= 0 \\\\ 2y &= -\\dfrac{14}{3} \\\\ y &= -\\dfrac{7}{3} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $y=-\\dfrac{7}{3}$ and\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{13}{3}\n$$\n\n, which can be written as\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{13}{3},-\\dfrac{7}{3}\\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505053 :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-6p + 4q + 14 &=0 \\\\ 2p + 4q + 6 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Sometimes, it's handy to assign a number to each equation in a system, so that we can refer to them more easily:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-6p + 4q + 14 &=0 && (1) \\\\ 2p + 4q + 6 &=0 && (2)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. Using equation $\\color{blue}(2)$, we can solve for $p$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 2p + 4q + 6 &= 0 \\\\ 2p + 4q &= -6 \\\\ 2p &= -6 - 4q \\\\ p &= \\dfrac{-6 - 4q}{2} \\\\ p &= -3 - 2q \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $p=-3-2q$ into equation $\\color{red}(1)$ and solve for $q$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} -6p + 4q + 14 &= 0 \\\\ -6(-3 - 2q) + 4q + 14 &= 0 \\\\ 18 + 12q + 4q + 14 &= 0 \\\\ 32 + 16q &= 0 \\\\ 16q &= -32 \\\\ q &= -2 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute\n\n$$\nq=-2\n$$\n\ninto equation $\\color{red}(1)$, then we can find the value of $p$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} -6p + 4q + 14 &= 0 \\\\ -6p + 4\\left(-2\\right) + 14 &= 0 \\\\ -6p - 8 + 14 &= 0 \\\\ -6p + 6 &= 0 \\\\ -6p &= -6 \\\\ p &= 1 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $p=1$ and\n\n$$\nq=-2\n$$\n\n, which can be written as\n\n$$\n\\left(1,-2\\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505059 :question/problem "Given the system of equations below, what is the value of $p - q$?\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2q + 16 &=0 \\\\ 2p + 4q + 28 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. Using equation $\\color{red}(1)$, we can solve for $q$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 2q + 16 &= 0 \\\\ 2q &= -16 \\\\ q &= -8 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $q=-8$ into equation $\\color{blue}(2)$ and solve for $p$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 2p + 4q + 28 &= 0 \\\\ 2p + 4\\left(-8\\right) + 28 &= 0 \\\\ 2p - 32 + 28 &= 0 \\\\ 2p &= 4 \\\\ p &= 2 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p - q = 2 - \\left(-8\\right) = 10$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505062 :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3u - 10v - 24 &=0 \\\\ 2u + 4v - 8 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Sometimes, it's handy to assign a number to each equation in a system, so that we can refer to them more easily:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3u - 10v - 24 &=0 && (1) \\\\ 2u + 4v - 8 &=0 && (2)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. Using equation $\\color{blue}(2)$, we can solve for $u$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 2u + 4v - 8 &= 0 \\\\ 2u + 4v &= 8 \\\\ 2u &= 8 - 4v \\\\ u &= \\dfrac{8 - 4v}{2} \\\\ u &= 4 - 2v \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $u=4-2v$ into equation $\\color{red}(1)$ and solve for $v$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 3u - 10v - 24 &= 0 \\\\ 3(4 - 2v) - 10v - 24 &= 0 \\\\ 12 - 6v - 10v - 24 &= 0 \\\\ -12 - 16v &= 0 \\\\ -16v &= 12 \\\\ v &= -\\dfrac{3}{4} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute\n\n$$\nv=-\\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\ninto equation $\\color{red}(1)$, then we can find the value of $u$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 3u - 10v - 24 &= 0 \\\\ 3u - 10\\left(-\\dfrac{3}{4}\\right) - 24 &= 0 \\\\ 3u + \\dfrac{15}{2} - 24 &= 0 \\\\ 3u - \\dfrac{33}{2} &= 0 \\\\ 3u &= \\dfrac{33}{2} \\\\ u &= \\dfrac{11}{2} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $u=\\dfrac{11}{2}$ and\n\n$$\nv=-\\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\n, which can be written as\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{11}{2},-\\dfrac{3}{4}\\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505067 :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-9x - 9y + 54 &=0 \\\\ 4x + 8y - 16 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Sometimes, it's handy to assign a number to each equation in a system, so that we can refer to them more easily:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-9x - 9y + 54 &=0 && (1) \\\\ 4x + 8y - 16 &=0 && (2)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. Using equation $\\color{blue}(2)$, we can solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 4x + 8y - 16 &= 0 \\\\ 4x + 8y &= 16 \\\\ 4x &= 16 - 8y \\\\ x &= \\dfrac{16 - 8y}{4} \\\\ x &= 4 - 2y \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=4-2y$ into equation $\\color{red}(1)$ and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} -9x - 9y + 54 &= 0 \\\\ -9(4 - 2y) - 9y + 54 &= 0 \\\\ -36 + 18y - 9y + 54 &= 0 \\\\ 18 + 9y &= 0 \\\\ 9y &= -18 \\\\ y &= -2 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute\n\n$$\ny=-2\n$$\n\ninto equation $\\color{red}(1)$, then we can find the value of $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} -9x - 9y + 54 &= 0 \\\\ -9x - 9\\left(-2\\right) + 54 &= 0 \\\\ -9x + 18 + 54 &= 0 \\\\ -9x + 72 &= 0 \\\\ -9x &= -72 \\\\ x &= 8 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=8$ and\n\n$$\ny=-2\n$$\n\n, which can be written as\n\n$$\n\\left(8,-2\\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505080 :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}7x - 7y - 35 &=0 \\\\ 2x - 4y - 6 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Sometimes, it's handy to assign a number to each equation in a system, so that we can refer to them more easily:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}7x - 7y - 35 &=0 && (1) \\\\ 2x - 4y - 6 &=0 && (2)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. Using equation $\\color{blue}(2)$, we can solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 2x - 4y - 6 &= 0 \\\\ 2x - 4y &= 6 \\\\ 2x &= 6 + 4y \\\\ x &= \\dfrac{6 + 4y}{2} \\\\ x &= 3 + 2y \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=3+2y$ into equation $\\color{red}(1)$ and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 7x - 7y - 35 &= 0 \\\\ 7(3 + 2y) - 7y - 35 &= 0 \\\\ 21 + 14y - 7y - 35 &= 0 \\\\ -14 + 7y &= 0 \\\\ 7y &= 14 \\\\ y &= 2 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute\n\n$$\ny=2\n$$\n\ninto equation $\\color{red}(1)$, then we can find the value of $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 7x - 7y - 35 &= 0 \\\\ 7x - 7\\left(2\\right) - 35 &= 0 \\\\ 7x - 14 - 35 &= 0 \\\\ 7x - 49 &= 0 \\\\ 7x &= 49 \\\\ x &= 7 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=7$ and\n\n$$\ny=2\n$$\n\n, which can be written as\n\n$$\n\\left(7,2\\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505081 :question/problem "Given the system of equations below, what is the value of $a - b$?\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4b + 32 &=0 \\\\ 5a + 2b - 19 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. Using equation $\\color{red}(1)$, we can solve for $b$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 4b + 32 &= 0 \\\\ 4b &= -32 \\\\ b &= -8 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $b=-8$ into equation $\\color{blue}(2)$ and solve for $a$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 5a + 2b - 19 &= 0 \\\\ 5a + 2\\left(-8\\right) - 19 &= 0 \\\\ 5a - 16 - 19 &= 0 \\\\ 5a &= 35 \\\\ a &= 7 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a - b = 7 - \\left(-8\\right) = 15$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505084 :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-10x - 10y + 7 &=0 \\\\ 4x + 8y - 16 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Sometimes, it's handy to assign a number to each equation in a system, so that we can refer to them more easily:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-10x - 10y + 7 &=0 && (1) \\\\ 4x + 8y - 16 &=0 && (2)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. Using equation $\\color{blue}(2)$, we can solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 4x + 8y - 16 &= 0 \\\\ 4x + 8y &= 16 \\\\ 4x &= 16 - 8y \\\\ x &= \\dfrac{16 - 8y}{4} \\\\ x &= 4 - 2y \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=4-2y$ into equation $\\color{red}(1)$ and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} -10x - 10y + 7 &= 0 \\\\ -10(4 - 2y) - 10y + 7 &= 0 \\\\ -40 + 20y - 10y + 7 &= 0 \\\\ -33 + 10y &= 0 \\\\ 10y &= 33 \\\\ y &= \\dfrac{33}{10} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{33}{10}\n$$\n\ninto equation $\\color{red}(1)$, then we can find the value of $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} -10x - 10y + 7 &= 0 \\\\ -10x - 10\\left(\\dfrac{33}{10}\\right) + 7 &= 0 \\\\ -10x - 33 + 7 &= 0 \\\\ -10x - 26 &= 0 \\\\ -10x &= 26 \\\\ x &= -\\dfrac{13}{5} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-\\dfrac{13}{5}$ and\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{33}{10}\n$$\n\n, which can be written as\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\dfrac{13}{5},\\dfrac{33}{10}\\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508059 :question/problem "![](images/4c/4c6a99f62a77f7fc16dbb95e8a8d03c42b266fffee10ed3739ab13820729be44.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the right-opening parabola shown above." :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola has its vertex at $O,$ and passes through the point $(2,-3).$\n\nThe standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2$ and $y=-3$ into the equation above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3)^{2} & =4p(2) \\\\ 9 & =8p \\\\ p & =\\frac{9}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of our parabola is \n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=\\frac{9}{2}x.\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{9}{2}x) \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{9}{4y}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508060 :question/problem "$\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}-1}=$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}-1}=\\frac{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}+1}-1}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}-1} & =\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}+1)}{(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}-1)(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}+1)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}+1)}{(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1})^{2}-{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}+1)}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}+1) & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}+1}+1 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1}+1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $2.$"}]}
